382:   As mentioned earlier, we cannot expect the stellar mass-to-light ratio to be constant across the entire galaxy, but there will likely be a radial variation in $\Upsilon_{\lambda}$ reflecting different star formation histories and stellar populations at different distances from the centre of NGC~300. A way to account for this radial variability of $\Upsilon_{\lambda}$ would be to determine the radial profile of the $J - K$ colour index of NGC~300 based on infrared imaging data. Unfortunately, the signal-to-noise ratio in the 2MASS images is very low, and determination of $\Upsilon_{\lambda}$ as a function of galactocentric distance is therefore not feasible due to large uncertainties.
